ozen funds, an end to sanctions and the lifting of the US naval blockade, conditional on restarting nuclear talks.","<b>27 Sep</b> \u2014 Trump rejected Iran's offer and would not rule out renewed strikes, closing off the one path that would have relieved oil quickly.","<b>28\u201329 Sep</b> \u2014 Brent peaked near $108 a barrel on the 28th, the highest since the Strait of Hormuz was shut in February, then fell to about $100 on the 29th as regional exports recovered to 12.8 million barrels a day in September (roughly 20 million before the war). Iran meanwhile claimed fresh attacks on shipping and US Navy vessels.","<b>29 Sep</b> \u2014 New York Fed president John Williams said there is no urgency for another rate increase after September's hike. The market-implied probability of an October increase fell from 69.6% to 35.0% in a day."],"today":["<b>No new geopolitical development was verified in this refresh.</b> This was a catch-up run that did not search the news, so the four sub-scores carry forward unchanged at Iran 4, Russia\u2013Ukraine 3, new theatre 3 and headline density 3 (total 13, ELEVATED)."]},"geoHist":{"2026-09-30":{"iran":4,"ru":3,"new":3,"density":3,"total":13,"flag":"ELEVATED","note":"Brent near $100 a barrel is now only about 9% above where it sat a month ago, down from roughly 18-20% above at the $108 peak two days ago, and that compression is the whole of what reaches crypto from this. The route runs oil into headline inflation, headline inflation into the expected path of policy rates, and the rate path into the price of dollar liquidity \u2014 it is not a sentiment channel, and the dashboard does not treat it as one. Today you can watch the entire chain complete in a single number: the market-implied probability of an October Fed increase fell from 69.6% yesterday to 35.0% today, which lifted the L4 liquidity indicator out of negative territory and took the liquidity bucket from -100 to -70. The caution is that this is a repricing of expectations rather than of cash: the Treasury and the Fed's reverse repo facility are still draining dollars faster than at any point this month.","today":["<b>The oil war premium is unwinding in earnest.</b> Brent settled near $100 a barrel on 29 September, down about 2.5% on the day and only around 9% above where it sat a month ago \u2014 against roughly 18\u201320% above a month ago when it peaked near $108 on the 28th. Bloomberg's 30 September market wrap has oil holding that loss, with Middle East exports now seen near pre-war levels. Supply is no longer arriving at a war price.","<b>That fed straight through to the Fed, and it is the biggest single thing on the board today.</b> New York Fed president John Williams said on 29 September that there is no urgency about another increase and no rush after September's hike, guiding towards just one more rise late in the year. The market-implied probability of an October increase fell from 69.6% to 35.0% in a day \u2014 the exact chain this section keeps describing, arriving in the one indicator that tracks it.","<b>All four sub-scores still carry forward unchanged</b> at Iran 4, Russia\u2013Ukraine 3, risk of a new theatre 3 and headline density 3, for a total of 13 and an ELEVATED flag. The premium is coming out of the oil price, but the blockade is still in force, Trump's rejection of Iran's terms still stands and Iran is still claiming attacks on shipping, so the conflict itself has not de-escalated even though the market's pricing of it has."]},"2026-10-01":{"iran":4,"ru":3,"new":3,"density":3,"total":13,"flag":"ELEVATED","note":"Oil is still the only route from the conflicts into crypto: oil feeds headline inflation, inflation feeds the expected path of Fed rates, and the rate path sets the price of dollar liquidity. Brent held near $100 a barrel, about a month's range below the $108 peak, and the market-implied chance of an October Fed increase is now 34.0%, down from 69.6% two days ago. That repricing is why the liqui